    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51127.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 0,
    "turn_text": "Banker podcast, I'm Neil Hagerty reporting from Washington. When North Dakota Senator Heidi Heidkamp first ran for the Senate in 2012, her bid to win as a Democrat in a traditionally conservative state was seen as a long shot. But she won, and this year she faces reelection for the first time and is pulling neck and neck with her Republican opponent, even though President Trump won North Dakota by more than 30 points. She pointed to her pragmatism and nonpartisan credentials as major sources of her success. One source of those credentials was a Senate bill that she co-sponsored that changed several key provisions in Dodd-Frank, including raising the minimum threshold for systemically risky banks and adjusting some home data reporting requirements. And she also has been a swing vote on some nominations that have come before the Senate Banking Committee. Speaking just a day before the committee voted on the nomination of Kathy Cranninger to head the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, Hite Camp told me she would vote against the nomination. She also talked to me about the process of crafting the Senate bill, her views on some other pending nominations, and what she thinks the Senate's priorities should be in the next Congress. Senator Hite Camp, good to be with you.",
