  {
    "episode_id": 51127,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51127.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "Well, we've really started this effort in 13, kind of working across the aisle. Back then, Senator Crapel was the ranking member, but we really started having these discussions on regulatory reform. And so what happened, you know, we were working within the Obama administration to try and get support from the administration from moving some of these provisions forward. I think if you look at those the first two years it really was building the kind of relationships that we need to build to achieve a package. Then we began that work again in the what I call the Shelby years but it was obvious that Shelby had his way and that we weren't going to be able to move anything if it wasn't the Shelby bill. But yet we continued to work on it and so when When President Trump was elected, I had known Gary Cohen for a number of years as he represented here in Washington. He would come in for Goldman Sachs. And so I called him and I said, you know, don't try and reinvent the wheel. This is what I think we can get done. We waited for negotiations between Senator Crapo and Senator Brown when those fell apart. We had a package. It's interesting because people said, boy, you pulled it together fast. I want to say, well, it was five years. I don't know that that's fast. So we knew functionally what we were able to do. But then again, working in a bipartisan way, trying to get more people signed on, we were able to expand beyond the four of us to achieve a much broader result.",
