    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 51127,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51127.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Well, we don't call it the Jobs Act. I call it the Capital Formations package, and obviously got broad bipartisan support. We need to get 60 votes over here. We're examining it as we promised Chairman Hensherling we would. So we're taking a look at all these provisions, and there's a number of provisions, consumer protection provisions that I think would be attractive on both sides of the aisle. So if we can do this bill, I think there's provisions where we wouldn't get 60 votes on some of the provisions that are in there. But if we can't identify those things, we can get done, maybe add some consumer protections, especially in the area of the credit bureaus. Everybody's still very concerned about what happened with Equifax. If we can fashion a package, we should try and do that. Now, at this point, I think if you said where is it, I think it really is in discussion with the ranking member and the chairman. And we continue to review it here as we have promised to do and continue to take a look at what that next opportunity would be to do that bill. So where I might support that bill in its entirety right now, I also like getting things done. And so I think it's a matter of understanding and appreciating that 60 votes isn't always easy to come by. And what could we get 60 votes for kind of moving forward?",
