  {
    "episode_id": 51127,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51127.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "I know one area that you've been particularly interested in, which you mentioned earlier, is housing finance reform. You know, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac have been under government conservatorship for about a decade. What do you think needs to happen with them?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "I am aware of your interest in housing finance reform.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac have been under government conservatorship for approximately ten years.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Housing finance reform was previously discussed in our conversation.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The current state of Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac's conservatorship has lasted for about a decade.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The listener values my recognition of their prior interests in the discussion.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The listener believes that Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac’s long-term conservatorship raises important policy questions.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "It is appropriate and expected to ask the listener about their opinion on structural changes to Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The listener considers the topic of housing finance reform relevant and worth discussing further.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Clarification Request (Specific)"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 51127,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51127.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "Well, we can't let them stay where they are. I mean, we've got to have a meaningful and realistic approach to GSE reform. You know, we tried Corker Warner slash Johnson Crapo, however we're going to label it. That seemed to be for a lot of people overly complicating. GSE reforms so now there's a number of proposals. I've had a long conversation with Senator Crapo who would love to get this issue resolved. But I think the one thing that my advice has been let's sit down and talk about what the problems are. What are we trying to fix in housing? And what I will tell you that I'm deeply concerned about that doesn't seem to get as much attention because we talk about GSE reform and we talk about kind of, you know, really resolving what is a 2008 problem, right? 2010 problem. Now we're over 10 years into this. We haven't addressed these concerns, but the biggest economic concern that I have is the lack of affordable housing. Why is that? What are we doing to fix that? And how can we help Americans with affordable housing? And I think that we can't just look at financing reform. We have to look at this as a major economic issue for American families. And so I think we need to fix this. We can't keep them in receivership. That's not smart. Therefore, a long time, a lot of we were taking on way too much taxpayer supported risk on housing, and that should be concerning to us all. We need to figure out how we can support a 30-year fixed rate mortgage with some kind of guarantee without taking all of the risk onto the taxpayers. And so I have concerns about taxpayer vulnerability. as it is right now, but I have equal concerns about what we're going to do on affordable housing.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "We cannot allow the current situation of the GSEs to continue.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Affordable housing is the speaker's primary economic concern regarding housing policy.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "A meaningful and realistic approach to GSE reform is necessary.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "GSEs currently operate under government receivership, which the speaker considers unwise.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Previous attempts at GSE reform, such as Corker-Warner and Johnson-Crapo, were perceived by many as overly complicated.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Over ten years have passed since the initial crisis without addressing key concerns.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "There are multiple ongoing proposals for GSE reform.",
