    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "Well, I don't know that it can get passed, but I think the credit bureaus are in a unique position. Number one, I didn't tell them that they could have my information. I don't have a relationship with them, but yet they have my information and they grade me. Well, I'd like to grade them back. And the grade right now isn't high. We were glad to see the charges on insider trading. That was really an important step for me. I thought that was particularly egregious. So I think getting their attention and making them aware that right now we literally have millions of files that have been co-opted by someone else, that's a ticking time bomb. I mean, people say, well, I haven't heard anything. They stole my file, but I haven't. You don't know that it's not going to explode on you. in five years, we don't know that. And so the breach having already happened, and then failure to report that breach almost immediately, and then doing insider trading before you reported it to the FBI, and then coming and saying, well, it's really not our fault. Those wouldn't recommend that we should cut anyone some slack. So we'll continue to take a look at it that, you know, like I said, the art of the possible. Are these things, are there things that we can get done to enhance consumer protection?",
